WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.800
Thousands of times a day, you breathe without thinking about it.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.800
Breathing is automatic.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.700
But it’s the one automatic rhythm you can also take over, any time you choose.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.000
You can’t steer your heartbeat or your stress hormones directly.

5
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.700
Your breath, you can. And it talks back to your brain.

6
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:33.800
Deep in the brainstem, a small cluster of cells keeps the rhythm.

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.600
In 2017, Stanford scientists found about 175 neurons there that report on breathing to the locus coeruleus, an arousal hub.

8
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.700
It sends signals across the whole brain.

9
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.400
When those neurons were removed, mice breathed more slowly and became unusually calm.

10
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.700
That was in mice, but humans have the same breathing pacemaker.

11
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.800
Breathing in changes what your brain notices.

12
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.200
In 2016, researchers recorded inside the brains of 7 people who had electrodes placed for epilepsy surgery.

13
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.700
With each in-breath through the nose, activity in the amygdala and hippocampus rose and fell.

14
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.600
In about 60 more people, fearful faces were spotted faster during an in-breath, and objects were remembered better.

15
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.700
Through the mouth, the effect faded.

16
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.400
Breathing out does something else: it slows your heart a little, every time.

17
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.700
A brake you carry everywhere.

18
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.800
In 2023, Stanford researchers had people practise for 5 minutes a day, for a month.

19
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.700
They compared three breathing techniques with mindfulness meditation.

20
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.000
The biggest mood boost came from an exhale-focused breath called the cyclic sigh.

21
00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.700
It also lowered resting breathing rate more than meditation did.

22
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.000
Try it with me.

23
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.200
Breathe in through your nose

24
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.200
One more short sip of air

25
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:12.200
Long, slow exhale through your mouth

26
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.200
Breathe in through your nose

27
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.200
One more short sip of air

28
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.200
Long, slow exhale through your mouth

29
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.200
Breathe in through your nose

30
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.200
One more short sip of air

31
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.600
Long, slow exhale through your mouth

32
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.800
Breathing in sharpens what you notice.

33
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.700
Breathing out slows you down.

34
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:49.800
Your breath is the one door into your nervous system
that you can open on purpose.
